  "_style": "Write standard, warm Chichewa (Chinyanja as used in Malawi), never cheesy. Always address the user politely with the plural/honorific “inu” forms (mu-/-ni verbs: “Sungani”, “Mukufuna…?”, “wanu/yanu/lanu”); address the couple as “inu awiri” or “nonse awiri”, and say “limodzi” for together. Refer to the other person as “wokondedwa wanu” (short form “wokondedwa” in tight labels), never mwamuna/mkazi/chibwenzi; Chichewa pronouns and verb agreement are already gender-neutral (“iye”, a-/wa- class 1), so keep them neutral and never add gendered nouns. When the user speaks to the partner inside a message or letter (e.g. reactions, pings), use intimate singular “iwe” forms (“Ndikukuganizira”, “Ndiimbire”). Quote marks: curly “ ” outside, ‘ ’ inside; use the single ellipsis character …, no space before ? ! : ; and keep “ · ”, \\n, emoji and all placeholders (%@, %lld, %1$@) exactly as in English. Numbers use Western digits, comma for thousands and period for decimals (1,250.5); dates follow the system date formatter, written order day month year (“14 Novembala 2026”). Buttons are short imperative verbs in the polite form (“Sungani”, “Tumizani kalata”, “Yesaninso”), with no “chonde” unless it is a full sentence; titles, tabs, chips and buttons use sentence case (only the first letter capitalized) except proper names like Coupley, Premium, AI. Latin script only; keep loanwords already common in Malawi (akaunti, intaneti, khodi, widget, Premium, AI) rather than inventing words. Plural categories are [\"one\",\"other\"]: “one” uses the singular noun (“tsiku %lld”), “other” the plural (“masiku %lld”), with the number placed after the noun as is natural in Chichewa. A partner's name placeholder (%@) is never changed with prefixes or locatives (no “kwa%@”, “a%@”): rephrase so the name stands alone, e.g. “%@ wakutumizirani kalata” or “Kalata yochokera kwa: %@”.",
